    idea: fact("A campaign in this repository searches for strategies, but it does not get to decide what counts as acceptable. Those decisions are written down first, in one document called the policy: fifteen lists of permitted names, nineteen permitted ranges for numbers, three limits on how large an expression may be, a handful of named references pinned to a fingerprint, and a map from each instrument to its business sector. A candidate reads its values out of that document and from nowhere else, so a name or a number the policy does not declare cannot be compiled at all. The policy is fingerprinted before the campaign opens, and that fingerprint is folded into every compiled plan and recorded against every trial. The search may read the policy but not write it, and a run may only be read against the policy it was judged under.", "tutorials/strategy-discovery/the-rules-nobody-may-choose/README.md", 17, "A campaign in this repository searches for strategies, but it does not get to decide what counts as\nacceptable. Those decisions are written down first, in one document called the policy: fifteen lists\nof permitted names, nineteen permitted ranges for numbers, three limits on how large an expression\nmay be, a handful of named references pinned to a fingerprint, and a map from each instrument to its\nbusiness sector. A candidate reads its values out of that document and from nowhere else, so a name\nor a number the policy does not declare cannot be compiled at all. The policy is fingerprinted\nbefore the campaign opens, and that fingerprint is folded into every compiled plan and recorded\nagainst every trial. The search may read the policy but not write it, and a run may only be read\nagainst the policy it was judged under.\n"),
