    idea: fact("When the discovery layer proposes a candidate strategy, it writes the candidate down as plain data before anything runs, and only later compiles that data into a plan and executes the plan. These are two different objects, and the layer gives each its own fingerprint, a short string of digits computed from the content, the same for the same content and different for any change. So a reader can tell the text that was submitted from the thing that actually ran, and neither fingerprint stands in for the other. More fingerprints sit on top: the frozen policy, the feature registry, the execution assumptions and the engine version, all mixed into the fingerprint of what ran, plus one naming the whole experiment. When a run finishes, the engine issues its own fingerprint for the result; the layer files one ledger entry keyed by the fingerprint of what ran and never rewrites it, and the campaign keeps a second append-only file, a chain in which each record carries the fingerprint of the record before it, so a removed or reordered record is visible against a copy of the chain's end kept elsewhere. Two things cannot be recomputed from what is kept, and this page states them as limits.", "tutorials/strategy-discovery/what-ran-versus-what-was-written/README.md", 17, "When the discovery layer proposes a candidate strategy, it writes the candidate down as plain data\nbefore anything runs, and only later compiles that data into a plan and executes the plan. These are\ntwo different objects, and the layer gives each its own fingerprint, a short string of digits computed\nfrom the content, the same for the same content and different for any change. So a reader can tell\nthe text that was submitted from the thing that actually ran, and neither fingerprint stands in for\nthe other. More fingerprints sit on top: the frozen policy, the feature registry, the execution\nassumptions and the engine version, all mixed into the fingerprint of what ran, plus one naming the\nwhole experiment. When a run finishes, the engine issues its own fingerprint for the result; the\nlayer files one ledger entry keyed by the fingerprint of what ran and never rewrites it, and the\ncampaign keeps a second append-only file, a chain in which each record carries the fingerprint of the\nrecord before it, so a removed or reordered record is visible against a copy of the chain's end kept\nelsewhere. Two things cannot be recomputed from what is kept, and this page states them as limits.\n"),
