    title: fact("Counting the search: the four counts a campaign keeps", "tutorials/strategy-discovery/counting-the-search/README.md", 1, "# Counting the search: the four counts a campaign keeps"),
    grade: fact("Strong as a definition: one versioned fold turns the campaign's own events into four counts, no method may define its own counting rule, the refusals in both directions are tested, and the correction's inputs are read back from the inventory rather than supplied by a caller; whether the resulting correction is well calibrated is a property of a live campaign, which is exactly why the overfitting probability is reported as a diagnostic and rejects nothing", "tutorials/strategy-discovery/counting-the-search/README.md", 12, " Strong as a definition: one versioned fold turns the campaign's own events into four counts, no method may define its own counting rule, the refusals in both directions are tested, and the correction's inputs are read back from the inventory rather than supplied by a caller; whether the resulting correction is well calibrated is a property of a live campaign, which is exactly why the overfitting probability is reported as a diagnostic and rejects nothing "),
    idea: fact("A search proposes many candidate strategies, and the same search runs them, so the number that a later correction needs is not the winner's score but how many attempts were made. This layer keeps one append-only record of everything a campaign does - every proposal, every compilation, every run that closes, every run admitted to the selection - and a single versioned fold turns that record into four separate counts. The counts are kept apart because a proposal is not a configuration, a configuration is not an execution, and an execution is not a statistical trial, and a method that needs a count reads it from this fold rather than from a number a caller hands it. Raw and effective counts are separate numbers: a campaign with dependent attempts must declare an effective count, and one with independent attempts must not. A diagnostic, the probability of backtest overfitting, is reported beside the counts but rejects nothing.", "tutorials/strategy-discovery/counting-the-search/README.md", 17, "A search proposes many candidate strategies, and the same search runs them, so the number that a later\ncorrection needs is not the winner's score but how many attempts were made. This layer keeps one\nappend-only record of everything a campaign does - every proposal, every compilation, every run that\ncloses, every run admitted to the selection - and a single versioned fold turns that record into four\nseparate counts. The counts are kept apart because a proposal is not a configuration, a configuration is\nnot an execution, and an execution is not a statistical trial, and a method that needs a count reads it\nfrom this fold rather than from a number a caller hands it. Raw and effective counts are separate\nnumbers: a campaign with dependent attempts must declare an effective count, and one with independent\nattempts must not. A diagnostic, the probability of backtest overfitting, is reported beside the counts\nbut rejects nothing.\n"),
    caveats: [
      fact("A single counter would carry several incompatible meanings at once. The number of ideas tried, the number of distinct configurations, the number of machine runs and the number of attempts that entered a comparison answer different questions, and a report that collapsed them could not be corrected.", "tutorials/strategy-discovery/counting-the-search/README.md", 216, "- A single counter would carry several incompatible meanings at once. The number of ideas tried, the\n  number of distinct configurations, the number of machine runs and the number of attempts that entered\n  a comparison answer different questions, and a report that collapsed them could not be corrected."),
      fact("The trial count can be forgotten or mis-declared. The correction needs the number of attempts, and a dependent campaign that declares no effective count is refused rather than given a default; a campaign that declares the wrong dependence gets a correction built on the wrong number.", "tutorials/strategy-discovery/counting-the-search/README.md", 219, "- The trial count can be forgotten or mis-declared. The correction needs the number of attempts, and a\n  dependent campaign that declares no effective count is refused rather than given a default; a campaign\n  that declares the wrong dependence gets a correction built on the wrong number."),
      fact("The refusal rule is a definitional choice, not a measurement. Counting refusals as trials would change every number, and the design states its choice rather than proving it.", "tutorials/strategy-discovery/counting-the-search/README.md", 222, "- The refusal rule is a definitional choice, not a measurement. Counting refusals as trials would change\n  every number, and the design states its choice rather than proving it."),
      fact("Digest identity catches the syntactic case and not the semantic one. A recompiled plan with identical behaviour, or a renamed family, has a different digest and could look like a new configuration; the policy states which differences count, and every merge is recorded rather than performed silently.", "tutorials/strategy-discovery/counting-the-search/README.md", 224, "- Digest identity catches the syntactic case and not the semantic one. A recompiled plan with identical\n  behaviour, or a renamed family, has a different digest and could look like a new configuration; the\n  policy states which differences count, and every merge is recorded rather than performed silently."),
      fact("The overfitting diagnostic is biased by an incomplete trial file. A guided search that reports only its converged outcomes lowers the value, and a value computed on a conveniently selected subset is worse than no value, because it carries the authority of a number. That is why the matrix is evaluated against its preconditions before it is computed.", "tutorials/strategy-discovery/counting-the-search/README.md", 227, "- The overfitting diagnostic is biased by an incomplete trial file. A guided search that reports only its\n  converged outcomes lowers the value, and a value computed on a conveniently selected subset is worse\n  than no value, because it carries the authority of a number. That is why the matrix is evaluated\n  against its preconditions before it is computed."),
      fact("The per-trial return series and Sharpe estimates are not read from the ledger at this milestone. An entry retains the engine's own document, which the Python side cannot deserialise, so the gate consumes those inputs as declared evidence; retaining a per-trial series is a later item rather than a change to the entries' identity.", "tutorials/strategy-discovery/counting-the-search/README.md", 231, "- The per-trial return series and Sharpe estimates are not read from the ledger at this milestone. An\n  entry retains the engine's own document, which the Python side cannot deserialise, so the gate consumes\n  those inputs as declared evidence; retaining a per-trial series is a later item rather than a change to\n  the entries' identity."),
    ],
